251
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:19,640
Hey, do you want to go out tonight?

252
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:22,650
No, I think I'll pass.

253
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:25,060
Mark broke up with me.

254
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:26,580
I feel awful.

255
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:27,320
What?

256
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:28,810
What happened?

257
00:12:29,670 --> 00:12:32,730
Just last week you were talking about going on vacation together.

258
00:12:33,390 --> 00:12:35,430
I don't know what I did wrong.

259
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:39,420
He said he needed some space to figure things out.

260
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:45,200
He said I didn't do anything wrong, that I'm a great person, just not the one for him.

261
00:12:46,280 --> 00:12:47,760
That's so cliche.

262
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:50,480
Mark's not worth your time, Lydia.

263
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,660
You deserve so much better.

264
00:12:53,590 --> 00:13:00,030
I know we had hit a rough patch, but I had hoped we could work it out next weekend when we both had some time off.

265
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:03,120
I think he found someone else.

266
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:07,680
I had been getting suspicious because he had been spending a lot of time with a co-worker.

267
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:08,680
Whatever.

268
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:09,680
He's a jerk.

269
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:10,680
Forget about him.

270
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:13,740
There are many more fish in the sea.

271
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:17,240
Talk about being cliche, Maggie.

272
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:19,240
But you're right.

273
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:21,560
There's no use for me to sulk around.

274
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:23,440
Let's go out tonight.

275
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:28,280
All right.

276
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:33,960
So talking about breakups, these cliche lines, and Maggie's obviously upset.

277
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:41,020
What other cliche lines or breakup phrases have you heard apart from, it's not you, it's me, you're just not the one.

278
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:44,540
I need some space.

279
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:47,540
I think of you more like a friend.

280
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:50,800
There's a boyfriend or girlfriend, but then there's a friend.

281
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:54,040
So listen, I like you, but I think of you more like a friend.

282
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:56,180
I think that's a typical one.

283
00:13:56,260 --> 00:14:03,600
Especially if you're asking someone out on a date, they might say that, oh, I really like you, but I see you more as a friend than anything else.

284
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:05,500
So there's another one that's very popular.

285
00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:10,060
Sometimes you hear people say, I'm just not ready for a relationship.

286
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:12,480
And that's also a cliche line.

287
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:13,900
Or I'm not ready to commit.

288
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:15,040
I'm not ready to commit.

289
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:20,400
And it's interesting because I think breaking up is one of the hardest things that people can do.

290
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:32,500
And I think maybe this is why you just use one of these phrases that are so popular and common because I guess it's kind of a good way to explain in very soft terms what you want to say and to break up with someone.

291
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:33,840
That's right.

292
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:36,420
And I think that sometimes people are afraid to make the other person sad.

293
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:42,200
And so they say these things that are very general, like it's not you, it's me.

294
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:48,680
But sometimes this makes the other person even more sad because they don't understand why that they're breaking up.

295
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:53,360
What about breaking up over the telephone or breaking up with a letter?

296
00:14:53,360 --> 00:15:03,280
Generally, at least in America, we say that breaking up over email or letter or phone is not a good way to end a relationship.

297
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:03,880
It's very cold.

298
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:04,740
It's very cold.

299
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:08,360
So the other person would be very angry if that happened.

300
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:08,800
That's right.

301
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:11,800
You just want to see the person and say, listen, let's have a conversation.

302
00:15:12,180 --> 00:15:13,800
Let's talk about this face to face.

303
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:14,620
Okay.

304
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:27,800
So this is actually very interesting because as we say, maybe in other countries or in other cultures, it's not really a negative thing to break up over the phone or maybe send an email saying, you know what, I don't want to see you anymore.

305
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:28,520
Right.

306
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:34,460
So I'd be very curious to hear about you guys and your cultures and what happens when people break up where you live.

307
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:35,580
So let us know.

308
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:37,920
Our website is EnglishPod.com.

309
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:38,600
We hope to see you there.

310
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,480
And of course, we hope that you haven't had any very bad breakup experiences.

311
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:44,960
But if you're comfortable sharing, we'd like to hear about your culture and your background.

312
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:45,960
All right.

313
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:46,260
So we'll see you guys there.

314
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:47,960
Bye.

315
00:15:47,960 --> 00:15:49,960
Bye.
